#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114944 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef75bc93-76da-5992-8285-181b808eb7a0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/id #uuid "79b049d8-e6ca-5c89-8e8d-89e937021df9" :question/math-academy-id "e-4118" :question/problem "What is the range of $y = 2(4)^x+3$?" :question/worked-solution "The range of $4^x$ is\n\n$$\n4^x \\gt 0\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $2$ and then adding $3$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4^{x} & > 0 \\\\ 2(4)^{x} & > 0 \\\\ 2(4)^{x} + 3 & > 0 + 3 \\\\ y & > 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $y \\in (3,\\infty)$.\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of\n\n$$\nf(x) = 2(4)^x+3\n$$\n\nis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1609/Source/Images/3f864041bbefdf05420e6ff37137cdb2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114947 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee02c223-dd7f-5c5d-b09f-e7bc6bd16b0c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Root of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/id #uuid "d3c10549-4149-5b51-8f1b-45f807e3cf62" :question/math-academy-id "e-4131" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x) = 3(2)^{x} - 48$." :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x)$, we must solve\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\n$$\n3(2)^{x} - 48 = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by adding $48$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3(2)^{x} - 48 &= 0 \\\\\n3(2)^{x} - 48 + 48 &= 0 + 48 \\\\\n3(2)^{x} &= 48\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3(2)^{x} &= 48 \\\\\n2^{x} &= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite $16$ as $2^4$ to get\n$2^x = 2^4$.\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(2)$, we equate the exponents. This gives\n$x = 4$.\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114948 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c2518d5-0fde-5dfc-aa07-f1bdd3c1585a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the End-Behavior of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/id #uuid "aa011085-4dbf-5688-bd25-f700595f36c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-4132" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x) = 3(e^{5x}) + 2$. Given that $f(x) \\to a$ as $x \\to -\\infty$, then what is the value of $a$?" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1609/Source/Images/4f7eca5fb85704c4c86b65a5d83bbab9.png)\n\nSince the function approaches the line $y=2$ as $x\\to -\\infty$, we conclude that\n$f(x)\\to 2 \\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to -\\infty$.\n\nTherefore, $a=2$.\n\nFinally, we note that the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ is $y=2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114949 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7df2ec3-6138-5b84-864b-a856c28b9747" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Y-Intercept of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/id #uuid "a8e00166-33ef-5676-9f3c-7f3c85b5fec7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4133" :question/problem "What is the $y$-intercept of $y = -2e^{-2x}+3$?" :question/worked-solution "To find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x = 0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -2e^{-2(0)} + 3 \\\\\n&=-2e^{0} + 3 \\\\\n&=-2(1) + 3 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $1$."}]}
